{#- Quadratic equation solved through the discriminant.

    The coefficients arrive already reduced to standard form: the blueprint's
    `derived` block computes a, b, c for whichever `structure` was rolled, so
    this template — like quad_disc.py — never needs to know whether they came
    from a hidden root pair, a factorisation, or a vertex form. One source of
    truth for the numbers, so the equation shown and the equation solved can
    never drift apart.

    Only the EXPANDED standard form is ever shown; the construction stays on
    this side of the wall. `a` is normalised positive by every branch of the
    `derived` block, so the macro only folds signs on b and c. -#}
{%- macro quad(a, b, c) -%}
{{ '' if a == 1 else a }}x^2
{%- if b != 0 %} {{ '+' if b > 0 else '-' }} {{ '' if b in (1, -1) else (b if b > 0 else -b) }}x{% endif %}
{%- if c != 0 %} {{ '+' if c > 0 else '-' }} {{ c if c > 0 else -c }}{% endif %} = 0
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- set counting = query_type == 'number_of_real_roots' -%}
PROBLEM TO POSE (turn ONLY this into the word problem; keep every number exactly as written):
- Equation: {{ quad(a, b, c) }}
{% if counting -%}
- Ask the student HOW MANY distinct real roots this equation has (сколько действительных корней).
{%- else -%}
- Ask the student to find every real root of this equation.
{%- endif %}

=== INTERNAL (guidance for you only — never show, restate, paraphrase, number, or hint any of this to the student) ===
- Method to recall: read off a, b, c from the standard form a x^2 + b x + c = 0, then
  compute the discriminant D = b^2 - 4ac and branch on its sign.
- D > 0 -> two distinct real roots; D = 0 -> one repeated root; D < 0 -> no real roots.
{% if not counting -%}
- Root formula: x = (-b ± sqrt(D)) / (2a). The denominator is 2a, NOT 2, and the
  numerator starts from -b, NOT b.
{% if structure == 'controlled_discriminant' -%}
- The discriminant here is deliberately controlled, so the roots may be irrational or
  may not exist at all. If they are irrational, the answer stays in exact radical form —
  do not ask for a decimal approximation. If D < 0 the honest answer is "нет действительных
  корней"; the problem must still read as a sensible question in that case.
{%- else -%}
- The coefficients were built so the roots come out clean; the student should still be
  routed through the discriminant, not through guessing a factorisation.
{%- endif %}
{%- else -%}
- Only the COUNT is asked for; the student needs the sign of D and nothing else, so the
  problem must not invite computing the roots themselves.
{%- endif %}
- The equation must be posed in the expanded standard form exactly as given above. Never
  present it factored, never mention that it was built from roots or linear factors, and
  never restate the vertex form.
- Render all math as LaTeX inside $...$ (e.g. ${{ quad(a, b, c) }}$).
- Do NOT state or hint at the answer: the value of the discriminant, the number of roots,
  and the roots themselves must NOT appear in the problem text.
